Preamble

The PhoenixLC 2.0 License is a free, protective license for software and 
other kinds of works.

The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed to take
away your freedom to share and change the works. By contrast, the PhoenixLC
2.0 License is intended to protect the original author's work while allowing
free use and distribution. The license ensures that the software remains free
for everyone, prevents unauthorized commercialization, and requires proper
attribution to the original author.

When we speak of free software under this license, we are referring to 
freedom from cost and freedom to use, not freedom to modify or redistribute 
modified versions. This license is designed to make sure that you have the 
freedom to use and distribute unmodified copies of the software for free, 
that you cannot sell the software or charge for it, that you cannot modify 
the core source code without permission, and that you know these limitations.

To protect the author's rights and ensure user safety, we need to prevent 
others from modifying the core code, selling the software, or claiming 
ownership. Therefore, you have certain responsibilities if you distribute 
copies of the software: responsibilities to respect the original author's 
work and to keep the software free for everyone.

For example, if you distribute copies of such a program, you must do so for 
free, you must pass on to the recipients the same freedoms that you received,
you must include the original license, and you must provide proper credit 
to the original author. You must make sure that recipients know the software
is free and cannot be sold.

Developers that use the PhoenixLC 2.0 License protect the author's rights 
with two steps: (1) assert copyright on the software, and (2) offer you this 
License giving you legal permission to use, copy, and distribute the software
in its unmodified form.

For the developers' and authors' protection, the PhoenixLC 2.0 License 
clearly explains that there is no warranty for this free software. For both 
users' and authors' sake, the license requires that modified versions must 
be approved by the author, so that their problems will not be attributed 
erroneously to authors of previous versions.

The PhoenixLC 2.0 License prohibits selling the software, charging for it, 
modifying the core code without permission, and distributing modified 
versions. This protects the integrity of the original work and ensures that 
all users receive the same reliable software.

Finally, every program is threatened constantly by unauthorized modifications
and commercialization. The PhoenixLC 2.0 License prevents these threats by 
clearly defining what is and isn't permitted, ensuring the software remains 
free and secure for everyone.

The precise terms and conditions for using, copying, distribution and 
modification follow.

TERMS AND CONDITIONS

0. Definitions.

"This License" refers to version 2.0 of the PhoenixLC License.

"Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds of
works, such as software, documentation, and other creative works.

"The Software" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this License.
Each licensee is addressed as "you". "Licensees" and "recipients" may be 
individuals or organizations.

To "modify" a work means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work in a 
fashion requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an exact 
copy. The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the earlier work 
or a work "based on" the earlier work.

A "covered work" means either the unmodified Software or a work based on the 
Software (with permission).

To "propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without permission, 
would make you directly or secondarily liable for infringement under 
applicable copyright law, except executing it on a computer or modifying a 
private copy. Propagation includes copying, distribution (with or without 
modification), making available to the public, and in some countries other 
activities as well.

To "convey" a work means any kind of propagation that enables other parties 
to make or receive copies. Mere interaction with a user through a computer 
network, with no transfer of a copy, is not conveying.

A "Contributor" is any person or entity that has been granted permission by 
the copyright holder to modify the Software.

"Core Code" means the fundamental source code, algorithms, architecture, and 
critical components of the Software that define its primary functionality.

"API Extensions" means plugins, add-ons, integrations, and other software 
that interacts with the Software's public interfaces without modifying the 
Core Code.

1. Grant of Rights.

You are permitted to use, copy, and distribute the Software in its original, 
unmodified form, subject to the conditions stated below.

You may make, run, and propagate covered works that you do not convey, 
without conditions so long as your license otherwise remains in force. You 
may convey covered works to others for the sole purpose of having them use 
the software, provided that you comply with the terms of this License in 
conveying all material for which you do not control copyright.

Conveying under any other circumstances is permitted solely under the 
conditions stated below. Sublicensing is allowed only under the same terms 
as this License.

2. Permitted Uses.

You may:
(a) Use the Software for any personal, educational, or commercial purpose
(b) Copy the Software for backup or archival purposes
(c) Distribute the Software in its original, unmodified form
(d) Create API Extensions that interact with the Software's interfaces
(e) Modify configuration files and settings
(f) Create plugins and add-ons that use the Software's API
(g) Integrate the Software with other applications
(h) Use the Software in research and development
(i) Use the Software in educational institutions
(j) Share the Software with others at no cost

3. Prohibited Uses.

You may NOT:
(a) Sell, charge, or monetize the Software in any form
(b) Modify the Core Code without explicit written permission
(c) Distribute modified versions of the Core Code
(d) Claim ownership of the Software
(e) Remove or alter copyright notices
(f) Use the Software in any product that competes directly with the original
(g) Create derivative works without permission
(h) Reverse engineer the Software
(i) Introduce security vulnerabilities
(j) Use the Software for illegal purposes

4. No Selling Clause.

You may NOT sell the Software in any form. This includes, but is not limited to:
(a) Direct sales of the Software
(b) Charging for downloads or access
(c) Subscription fees for using the Software
(d) Selling as part of a bundle or package
(e) Paid licensing fees
(f) Commercial licensing of the Software
(g) Selling access to the Software (SaaS)
(h) Charging for support or maintenance

The Software must remain FREE for everyone. No exceptions.

5. Forced Credit Clause.

You MUST provide credit to the original author in ALL circumstances:

(a) When using the Software
(b) When distributing the Software
(c) When creating API Extensions
(d) When building applications on top of the Software
(e) When mentioning the Software in any context
(f) In all documentation
(g) In all promotional materials
(h) In all user interfaces

Required Credit Format:
"This software uses SuperSRT (PhoenixLC 2.0) developed by DAPOWER99
 (https://github.com/DAPOWER99/SuperSRT)
 Copyright © 2026 DAPOWER99"

Credit must be prominent, visible, and easily discoverable. Failure to 
provide proper credit is a violation of this license.

6. Contributor Permission Clause.

You may request permission from the owner (DAPOWER99) to modify the Core Code.

(a) Submit a request via GitHub Issues
(b) Explain the proposed modifications
(c) Explain the purpose and benefits
(d) Provide a timeline for completion
(e) Sign a Contributor License Agreement (CLA)

The owner may grant permission at their sole discretion. Permission may be 
granted on a case-by-case basis. Contributors must:
(a) Provide proper credit to the original author
(b) Document all changes made
(c) Not remove or alter copyright notices
(d) Not claim ownership of the Software
(e) Not sell or monetize their modifications
(f) Contribute modifications back to the project

7. Code Protection Clause.

The Core Code may NOT be modified without permission to ensure:
(a) User safety and reliability
(b) Prevention of security vulnerabilities
(c) Maintenance of software integrity
(d) Consistent behavior across all installations
(e) Protection of users from malicious code
(f) Prevention of introduction of bugs

API Extensions are permitted and do not require permission, provided they:
(a) Do not modify the Core Code
(b) Use official API interfaces
(c) Provide proper credit
(d) Do not create security vulnerabilities

8. Modification Restrictions.

You may NOT modify the Core Code without permission, including but not 
limited to:
(a) Fundamental source code files
(b) Critical algorithms
(c) Core architecture
(d) Security-critical components
(e) Authentication mechanisms

You may modify (without permission):
(a) Configuration settings
(b) Plugin behavior
(c) Integration points
(d) User interface extensions
(e) Workflow customizations

9. Distribution Requirements.

When distributing the Software, you must:
(a) Include this complete license agreement
(b) Retain all copyright notices
(c) Not claim ownership
(d) Distribute for FREE
(e) Provide proper credit
(f) Include the original documentation

You may NOT distribute modified versions without permission.

10. Attribution Requirements.

Must always include:
(a) Copyright notice in all copies
(b) License text with all distributions
(c) Author credit in all documentation
(d) Link to the original repository
(e) Notice of use in any product
(f) The full license text

